WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.826
Welcome to Module 6 of
the Data Platform and SQL

2
00:00:03.826 --> 00:00:07.375
Server Identity learning
path: NTLM and the SPNs.

3
00:00:07.375 --> 00:00:11.851
It goes with Runbook R oh six and
Chapter 06 of the developer workbook,

4
00:00:11.851 --> 00:00:14.075
Building SQL Server Identity.

5
00:00:14.075 --> 00:00:19.113
You'll find every reason an instance's
connections use NTLM or fail Kerberos,

6
00:00:19.113 --> 00:00:22.712
and write each S P N fix for
review instead of running it.

7
00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:25.880
Take this module after Modules 5 and 2.

8
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.017
Why does this matter?

9
00:00:31.017 --> 00:00:33.429
You can't remove NTLM you haven't found,

10
00:00:33.429 --> 00:00:37.004
and most of what you find is
a missing or misplaced name.

11
00:00:37.004 --> 00:00:40.043
Windows Authentication
tries Kerberos first.

12
00:00:40.043 --> 00:00:44.868
When the client can't get a ticket for
the instance's S P N, it uses NTLM.

13
00:00:44.868 --> 00:00:49.455
And when the S P N sits on the wrong
account, authentication fails outright.

14
00:00:49.455 --> 00:00:51.941
In this module you list, per instance,

15
00:00:51.941 --> 00:00:56.664
every reason its connections use NTLM
or fail Kerberos, with the set S

16
00:00:56.664 --> 00:01:01.370
P N line that would fix each S P N
problem, ready for a change window.

17
00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.886
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

18
00:01:07.886 --> 00:01:10.170
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

19
00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.353
You'll name the patterns that make
sequel server connections use NTLM,

20
00:01:14.353 --> 00:01:17.857
and the two S P N problems that
fail instead of falling back.

21
00:01:17.857 --> 00:01:21.120
You'll explain why local connections
are left out of the count.

22
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.303
You'll run the kit's
NTLM audit over fixtures

23
00:01:24.303 --> 00:01:27.133
on day 1 and day 30 and read its findings.

24
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:30.469
And you'll read the fix file and
say why nothing in it has run.

25
00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:33.610
Before you start, finish
Module 5, and Module 2,

26
00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.495
whose audit snapshots this module reads.

27
00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:42.260
The lab runs in PowerShell 7, in the kit
you unzipped into the F M W Labs folder.

28
00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.435
Start with what Microsoft Learn gives you.

29
00:01:48.435 --> 00:01:51.023
The client builds an S
P N for the instance.

30
00:01:51.023 --> 00:01:55.395
For T C P it's M S SQL S V C,
slash, the fully qualified name,

31
00:01:55.395 --> 00:02:00.434
colon, the port, registered on the
account the service runs under.

32
00:02:00.434 --> 00:02:03.623
Learn's table of S P N
states is the triage key.

33
00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:07.365
When the S P N maps to the
account the service runs under,

34
00:02:07.365 --> 00:02:11.273
local connections use NTLM
and remote ones use Kerberos.

35
00:02:11.273 --> 00:02:14.722
When it maps to an incorrect
account, authentication fails.

36
00:02:14.722 --> 00:02:17.598
And when the lookup fails,
or there's no S P N,

37
00:02:17.598 --> 00:02:20.644
local and remote
connections both use NTLM.

38
00:02:20.644 --> 00:02:26.228
Notice the first row: a connection made
on the server itself always reports NTLM,

39
00:02:26.228 --> 00:02:28.935
even when Kerberos is set up correctly.

40
00:02:28.935 --> 00:02:31.640
So you judge only remote connections.

41
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.277
Unit 2 is the patterns.

42
00:02:37.277 --> 00:02:42.377
The data platform book's chapter on NTLM
names four patterns at the sequel layer.

43
00:02:42.377 --> 00:02:44.088
A missing or incorrect S P N.

44
00:02:44.088 --> 00:02:47.977
Cross-site connections that
can't reach a domain controller.

45
00:02:47.977 --> 00:02:49.515
Linked-server passthrough.

46
00:02:49.515 --> 00:02:51.577
And clients that aren't domain-joined.

47
00:02:51.577 --> 00:02:56.302
Its triage point matters most: an
S P N on the wrong account doesn't

48
00:02:56.302 --> 00:03:01.115
fall back to NTLM, it fails, and a
duplicate S P N breaks Kerberos too.

49
00:03:01.115 --> 00:03:06.477
That shows up as an error, not as NTLM, so
it gets triaged as a different problem.

50
00:03:06.477 --> 00:03:10.422
And a linked server that passes
the caller's credentials needs

51
00:03:10.422 --> 00:03:14.450
Kerberos delegation, because
NTLM can't make the second hop.

52
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.800
Unit 3 is discovery first,
and fixes for review.

53
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.949
The data platform book's three phases of
NTLM elimination start with discovery:

54
00:03:26.949 --> 00:03:30.100
the baseline that later
phases measure against.

55
00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.440
The kit's NTLM audit joins the
audit snapshot, the estate,

56
00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:40.100
the domain's M S SQL S V C S P Ns, the
linked servers and the client records.

57
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.631
It builds the expected
T C P S P N for each

58
00:03:42.631 --> 00:03:45.400
instance and compares
it with who holds it.

59
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.857
For a virtual or built-in service account,

60
00:03:47.857 --> 00:03:50.387
it expects the S P N on
the computer account.

61
00:03:50.387 --> 00:03:54.699
It writes each set S P N fix to a
file for review, and never runs it.

62
00:03:54.699 --> 00:03:58.650
Runbook R oh six puts each change
in a window, with its back-out.

63
00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.462
Two patterns aren't detected.

64
00:04:00.462 --> 00:04:03.992
Cross-site connections look like
a missing S P N in this data,

65
00:04:03.992 --> 00:04:06.874
so confirm with K list
before changing anything.

66
00:04:06.874 --> 00:04:10.430
And only the T C P form
of the S P N is checked.

67
00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:18.780
Chapter 06 of the workbook names the
dangers, and the guard against each.

68
00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:23.645
Running the fix file as a script moves
every S P N in it at once, outside review;

69
00:04:23.645 --> 00:04:28.005
the guard is one reviewed line at a
time, in a window, with its back-out.

70
00:04:28.005 --> 00:04:30.236
For a virtual or built-in service account,

71
00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:33.912
the S P N belongs on the computer
account, and moving it to the service

72
00:04:33.912 --> 00:04:38.243
account would break Kerberos, so the kit
expects the computer account for those.

73
00:04:38.243 --> 00:04:43.612
Cross-site NTLM read as a missing S P N
leads to an S P N that changes nothing;

74
00:04:43.612 --> 00:04:47.280
run K list get from the
client's site before any fix.

75
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.228
A linked server fixed with unconstrained
delegation leaves the first server holding

76
00:04:52.228 --> 00:04:56.213
a reusable ticket for every caller;
Runbook R oh six's options are Entra

77
00:04:56.213 --> 00:05:01.092
authentication, constrained delegation, or
a mapped login as a documented exception.

78
00:05:01.092 --> 00:05:03.888
And because only the T
C P S P N is checked,

79
00:05:03.888 --> 00:05:09.970
named pipes or short-name clients can still
use NTLM, so check those forms by hand.

80
00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:14.657
Try it.

81
00:05:14.657 --> 00:05:19.208
Lab 1 runs in PowerShell 7 on the
kit's fictional estate, with no tenant.

82
00:05:19.208 --> 00:05:22.894
Go to the kit's folder, and
make this run's results folder,

83
00:05:22.894 --> 00:05:27.482
named for the time you start, in U T
C, so every run keeps its own results.

84
00:05:27.482 --> 00:05:31.757
Step one runs the audit on day 1:
seven findings on four instances.

85
00:05:31.757 --> 00:05:37.632
SQL P R D oh two is missing its S P N,
so remote connections fall back to NTLM.

86
00:05:37.632 --> 00:05:41.340
On SQL F C I oh four, the S P
N is held by an old account,

87
00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:45.307
not the one the service runs
as, so authentication fails.

88
00:05:45.307 --> 00:05:50.619
On SQL P R D oh six, two accounts
hold it, so Kerberos can't choose.

89
00:05:50.619 --> 00:05:53.770
Then there's remote NTLM,
a linked server passing

90
00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:57.157
the caller's credentials,
and a client off the domain.

91
00:05:57.157 --> 00:06:03.332
Look at SQL P R D oh one: its S P N is
correct, yet forty-one sessions use NTLM.

92
00:06:03.332 --> 00:06:08.808
The client file shows that host is
domain-joined, so look on the client side:

93
00:06:08.808 --> 00:06:12.630
aliases, hosts file entries,
D N S and old drivers.

94
00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:21.105
Step two reads the fix file: four lines
for three instances, and nothing has run.

95
00:06:21.105 --> 00:06:23.597
The first line says so: review each line,

96
00:06:23.597 --> 00:06:27.092
and run it with Domain Admin
rights in a change window.

97
00:06:27.092 --> 00:06:31.180
SQL P R D oh two gets its S P
N added on its service account.

98
00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:35.034
On SQL F C I oh four, the S P N
is deleted from the old account,

99
00:06:35.034 --> 00:06:37.630
then added to the one the service runs as.

100
00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:41.285
And on SQL P R D oh six, the
S P N stays with the G M S

101
00:06:41.285 --> 00:06:45.305
A the service runs as, and
leaves the computer account.

102
00:06:45.305 --> 00:06:49.158
That's the guard against a danger:
a duplicate removed from the wrong

103
00:06:49.158 --> 00:06:53.520
account takes the S P N away from the
service account, and Kerberos stops.

104
00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:02.232
Step three reads every fix, including
the ones that aren't set S P N lines.

105
00:07:02.232 --> 00:07:06.600
For remote NTLM, the fix is to find
each client, from the client rows or

106
00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.333
the client address in the connections
view; local sessions are excluded.

107
00:07:11.333 --> 00:07:16.196
For the linked-server passthrough on SQL
P R D oh one, it's constrained delegation,

108
00:07:16.196 --> 00:07:18.663
a mapped login, or the
sequel server twenty

109
00:07:18.663 --> 00:07:21.271
twenty-five managed-identity
linked server.

110
00:07:21.271 --> 00:07:25.310
And for the client off the domain, it's
moving Reports Readers to an Entra

111
00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:29.582
login with a token-based connection,
which is the next module's subject.

112
00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:36.830
Step four runs the same
audit on the day-30 snapshot.

113
00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:39.936
SQL P R D oh one's remote NTLM is gone:

114
00:07:39.936 --> 00:07:44.517
its logins moved to Entra in
Chapter 04 of the workbook.

115
00:07:44.517 --> 00:07:48.051
But the S P N problems on the
other instances are still there,

116
00:07:48.051 --> 00:07:49.781
because nobody ran the fixes.

117
00:07:49.781 --> 00:07:52.355
The audit finds; it doesn't repair.

118
00:07:52.355 --> 00:07:55.318
The linked-server and client
findings come from the

119
00:07:55.318 --> 00:07:58.590
same fixture files on both
days, so they're unchanged.

120
00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:04.578
Step five opens the manifest.

121
00:08:04.578 --> 00:08:07.902
It names the step, says
the run was read-only,

122
00:08:07.902 --> 00:08:11.952
and lists the controls, I A two
and C M six, with one K S I.

123
00:08:11.952 --> 00:08:16.364
Then the S H A two fifty-six of
each file, the fix file included.

124
00:08:16.364 --> 00:08:19.503
That's the point: the fix file
is hashed with the findings,

125
00:08:19.503 --> 00:08:22.777
so the lines a change board
approves are the lines that run.

126
00:08:22.777 --> 00:08:26.953
Step six runs this chapter's
tests: seven pass, none fail.

127
00:08:26.953 --> 00:08:28.828
And step seven lists your runs.

128
00:08:28.828 --> 00:08:32.237
There's nothing to clean up: the
kit's folder is as you unzipped it,

129
00:08:32.237 --> 00:08:35.040
and each run's results
stay in their own folder.

130
00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:43.603
Lab 2 runs the audit on your own test
domain and test instance, never production.

131
00:08:43.603 --> 00:08:45.502
It was not run by the authors.

132
00:08:45.502 --> 00:08:50.714
You need PowerShell 7 on a domain-joined
machine that isn't the sequel server host,

133
00:08:50.714 --> 00:08:54.913
because a connection made on the
host itself always reports NTLM.

134
00:08:54.913 --> 00:08:58.879
Steps one to five only read: query
the domain's S P Ns with set S P N,

135
00:08:58.879 --> 00:09:03.390
snapshot the instance, write the
audit's other inputs, and run the audit.

136
00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.253
Step six is a change.

137
00:09:05.253 --> 00:09:08.278
Run the one reviewed fix
only in an approved window,

138
00:09:08.278 --> 00:09:12.890
with its back-out line ready, then check
the auth scheme and K list from a client.

139
00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:17.482
If the window's checks fail, the set S
P N delete line restores the old state,

140
00:09:17.482 --> 00:09:20.700
and step seven deletes
the lab's output folder.

141
00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.100
The federal requirement.

142
00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:30.589
Chapter 06 of the workbook maps
the audit and fix plan to C M six,

143
00:09:30.589 --> 00:09:37.975
Configuration Settings, in NIST SP
800-53, with the K S I S V C A C M.

144
00:09:37.975 --> 00:09:42.025
The evidence is the findings file
and the fix file with the manifest,

145
00:09:42.025 --> 00:09:45.063
and the change record for
each set S P N line that ran.

146
00:09:45.063 --> 00:09:49.509
It maps the same plan to I A two,
Identification and Authentication

147
00:09:49.509 --> 00:09:53.551
for organizational users, which
has no K S I pairing in the kit.

148
00:09:53.551 --> 00:09:55.606
Its evidence is the same findings,

149
00:09:55.606 --> 00:09:59.938
and the auth scheme and K list record
from a remote client after each fix.

150
00:09:59.938 --> 00:10:03.713
Runbook R oh six lists the evidence
for each remediation state.

151
00:10:03.713 --> 00:10:07.940
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers;

152
00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:11.400
your providers report against
them, the agency doesn't.

153
00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:15.361
The kit's records support these
controls and provide evidence for them;

154
00:10:15.361 --> 00:10:18.413
the assessor decides whether
each one is in place.

155
00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:24.260
Check yourself with two questions.

156
00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:28.596
First: on SQL F C I oh four, the
S P N is held by the old account,

157
00:10:28.596 --> 00:10:31.460
but the service runs as its own account.

158
00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.473
What happens to remote connections?

159
00:10:33.473 --> 00:10:36.348
The answer is b: authentication fails.

160
00:10:36.348 --> 00:10:40.736
An S P N on the wrong account fails
rather than falls back to NTLM.

161
00:10:40.736 --> 00:10:44.847
Second: what has happened to the lines
in the fix file when the audit finishes?

162
00:10:44.847 --> 00:10:47.823
The answer is b: nothing
in the file has run.

163
00:10:47.823 --> 00:10:51.710
Each line is reviewed and run in a
change window, with its back-out.

164
00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:56.350
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

165
00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:01.175
To sum up.

166
00:11:01.175 --> 00:11:05.408
You found the missing, misplaced and
duplicate S P Ns, the remote NTLM,

167
00:11:05.408 --> 00:11:10.762
the linked-server passthrough and the client
off the domain in the fictional estate.

168
00:11:10.762 --> 00:11:15.276
And you saw that the S P N problems stay
until someone runs the reviewed fixes.

169
00:11:15.276 --> 00:11:17.510
The fix file is hashed with the findings,

170
00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:20.762
so the lines a change board
approves are the lines that run.

171
00:11:20.762 --> 00:11:24.796
The I S S O keeps the findings and
the fix file with the manifest,

172
00:11:24.796 --> 00:11:27.882
the change record for each
set S P N line that ran,

173
00:11:27.882 --> 00:11:32.549
and the auth scheme and K list record
from a remote client after each fix.

174
00:11:32.549 --> 00:11:36.025
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 06 checklist.

